{"record": "end", "run_id": "20261116T082414Z_dev-02_staged_2d82", "reason": "answered", "detail": "", "answer": {"answer": "No. The two analyses use different thresholds. The dSph gamma-ray limit is a one-sided 95% upper limit, set where 2[ln L_max − ln L] = 2.71 (a TS drop of 2.71). The AMS-02 antiproton limit is the upper endpoint of the ΔQ ≤ 3.84 region, where ΔQ is the rise in −2 ln L. The paper says this 3.84 is the nominal two-sided 95% interval threshold for one parameter of interest, and that it differs from the usual one-sided 2.71 prescription.\n\nThe nuisance parameters also differ. The dSph analysis profiles over each dwarf's J-factor. It does this through an energy-independent normalization s (J = s·J0) with a Gaussian prior in log10 J, profiled over log10 s in [−3, 3]. The antiproton analysis profiles over the Galactic diffusive-halo half-height L. It uses a Gaussian penalty in log10 L, centred on 4.96 kpc with σ = 0.197, profiled over 1–12 kpc.", "found": true, "evidence": [{"arxiv_id": "2610.26648v1", "section_id": "4.2"}, {"arxiv_id": "2610.26648v1", "section_id": "4.3"}, {"arxiv_id": "2610.26648v1", "section_id": "5"}]}, "turns": 5, "seconds": 10.145, "tool_calls": 5, "tool_result_chars": 22668, "input_tokens": 12, "output_tokens": 846, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 3854, "cache_read_input_tokens": 33768, "parser_versions": ["4"], "response_models": ["claude-sonnet-5-5"], "ended_at": "2026-11-16T08:24:25+00:00"}
